WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.250
Picture a team of eight engineers,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.930
all running coding agents,

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.590
with nobody watching the meter.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.020
Then the first real bill arrives,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.110
$2 ,200,

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.540
for one month.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.260
Meanwhile,

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.470
at a healthcare company,

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.580
patient data can't touch an outside server

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:19.080
at all,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.780
so the only option is a local

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.630
model.

00:00:22.118 --> 00:00:24.168
Both stories ask the same question:

00:00:24.198 --> 00:00:25.908
where does the model run?

00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:27.788
It sounds like infrastructure.

00:00:27.998 --> 00:00:30.138
It's really a decision about trust,

00:00:30.138 --> 00:00:30.718
cost,

00:00:30.718 --> 00:00:31.568
and capability.

00:00:32.575 --> 00:00:34.795
Commercial frontier models are where the best

00:00:34.795 --> 00:00:35.875
reasoning lives.

00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:38.135
They call tools cleanly and stay inside

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:38.955
the lines,

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:40.595
deep into a long session.

00:00:40.595 --> 00:00:42.795
But even a huge context window has

00:00:42.795 --> 00:00:43.415
limits.

00:00:43.415 --> 00:00:45.215
An agent at eighty percent of its

00:00:45.215 --> 00:00:47.235
window reasons worse than the same agent

00:00:47.235 --> 00:00:48.145
at twenty.

00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:50.944
Local models give you something no API

00:00:50.944 --> 00:00:51.544
can,

00:00:51.544 --> 00:00:53.454
your code never leaves the machine,

00:00:53.504 --> 00:00:54.874
and every token is free.

00:00:55.084 --> 00:00:57.384
Open models got much better at agentic

00:00:57.384 --> 00:00:57.844
work.

00:00:57.844 --> 00:00:59.714
But be honest about the hardware.

00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:01.364
You need serious memory,

00:01:01.364 --> 00:01:02.124
and on long,

00:01:02.124 --> 00:01:03.504
multi -file tasks,

00:01:03.504 --> 00:01:05.074
smaller models drift,

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:06.274
and lose the plan.

00:01:07.106 --> 00:01:09.336
Agents burn tokens because they're working.

00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:11.886
Every step resends the growing conversation,

00:01:11.886 --> 00:01:13.806
so a simple bug fix can run

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:15.756
to tens of thousands of tokens.

00:01:15.866 --> 00:01:17.306
So set limits,

00:01:17.306 --> 00:01:18.836
scope tasks tightly,

00:01:19.086 --> 00:01:20.506
cap turns per task,

00:01:20.506 --> 00:01:23.136
and put hard stops on anything automated.

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:25.226
A pipeline agent stuck in a retry

00:01:25.226 --> 00:01:26.506
loop at 3 a .m.

00:01:26.506 --> 00:01:28.736
is exactly how the surprise bill happens.

00:01:29.591 --> 00:01:31.441
and measure the return honestly.

00:01:31.471 --> 00:01:32.491
In one trial,

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:35.211
experienced developers were slower with AI tools

00:01:35.211 --> 00:01:37.021
while believing they were faster.

00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:39.541
How fast it feels is not evidence.

00:01:39.671 --> 00:01:42.061
Track lead time and failure rates instead.

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:45.389
Some code legally cannot leave the building.

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:46.819
Government contracts,

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:47.959
patient records,

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:50.529
financial systems… For that code,

00:01:50.529 --> 00:01:52.089
a local model isn't a nice -to

00:01:52.089 --> 00:01:52.529
-have.

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:53.929
It's the only option.

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:55.209
There's a middle path,

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:55.929
too.

00:01:55.929 --> 00:01:58.229
Frontier models deployed inside your own cloud

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:00.379
boundary with your data kept there.

00:02:01.091 --> 00:02:03.211
So the real question isn't local or

00:02:03.211 --> 00:02:03.931
commercial.

00:02:03.931 --> 00:02:06.261
It's which model for which step?

00:02:06.531 --> 00:02:08.531
Send exploration to a small,

00:02:08.531 --> 00:02:09.391
cheap model.

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:11.271
Save the frontier model for planning,

00:02:11.271 --> 00:02:13.221
where reasoning matters most.

00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:15.571
Hand implementation to a mid -tier model

00:02:15.571 --> 00:02:17.021
that follows the plan.

00:02:17.828 --> 00:02:19.678
Don't get religious about models.

00:02:19.708 --> 00:02:21.688
The best engineers are pragmatists.

00:02:21.688 --> 00:02:23.728
They measure what works and switch when

00:02:23.728 --> 00:02:25.128
something better comes along.

00:02:25.128 --> 00:02:26.828
The model is a tool and the

00:02:26.828 --> 00:02:28.408
skill is knowing which one to reach

00:02:28.408 --> 00:02:29.108
for.

00:02:29.108 --> 00:02:29.978
Next up,

00:02:30.128 --> 00:02:32.888
Multi -Agent Orchestration When one agent isn't

00:02:32.888 --> 00:02:33.738
enough.
